22.13.0
# ^ The version MUST stay on line 1: actions/setup-node reads the first line of .nvmrc and does not skip comments.
#   An earlier version of this file began with a '# comment' line, which made setup-node resolve the version as "#"
#   and fail with "Unable to find Node version '#'". Comments below the version are safe; comments above are not.
#
# Why 22.13.0 specifically (three independent reasons, discovered after CI went red on its first eleven runs):
#
#   1. electron/services/legacy-import.ts imports `DatabaseSync` from `node:sqlite`. The module first appeared in Node
#      22.5.0 behind `--experimental-sqlite`; from 22.13.0 it works without the flag. On Node 20 the import does not
#      degrade, it throws: "ERR_UNKNOWN_BUILTIN_MODULE: No such built-in module: node:sqlite".
#   2. Several dependencies declare `engines.node >= 22.12.0`, which npm reported as EBADENGINE warnings on every
#      install — the answer was in the CI log the whole time.
#   3. The original value here was 20, copied from the README rather than derived from the imports. The README was
#      wrong and this file faithfully repeated it.
#
# The split that makes this easy to miss: the *application* runs on Electron's bundled Node (currently 24.x), so a
# Node 20 machine builds and runs the app perfectly. Only the unit suite and build scripts that import application
# modules execute on the host Node. That is exactly the class of failure CI exists to catch.
